{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nADR 0049 (`docs/adr/0049-opcert-asymmetric.md`) pins trusted op-cert environment keys in the repository file `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml`, and ADR 0104 (`docs/adr/0104-mcp-on-box.md`, decision 3 and its env_id note) builds the MCP server's enforcement plan on that pin file. The operator decided on 2026-11-19 (epic nonblack-brigandish-guillemot) that op-cert keys must not be hardcoded in the repository. This task records that decision as a new ADR before any code changes, so the later stories implement a written decision.\n\n## Approach\nWrite `docs/adr/0125-opcert-trust-per-environment.md` and reserve the number with the marker file `docs/adr/.numbers/0125` (content `0125-opcert-trust-per-environment.md`, as `docs/adr/.numbers/0124` holds its slug); `scripts/check_adr_numbers.py` (run by `make lint`) requires it, and its add/add conflict blocks a duplicate claim at merge. Regenerate `docs/adr/README.md` with `python scripts/gen_adr_index.py` rather than hand-editing it. Header shape follows ADR 0124 (Status, Date, Ticket, Epic); sections Context, Decision, Consequences, Alternatives rejected. Context describes today's trust root (own key, then pinned key, then embedded key; bug c21f) and its problems. The Decision records:\n1. Principle: identity, authorization and op-cert certification are separate. Anyone can sign a plan review, code review or completion verification; signing is never gated on an allowed identity or key.\n2. Default verification is validity-only: the SSHSIG signature verifies under the key embedded in it, for the certificate's principal and the `rebar.opcert.v1` namespace, and the signed subject binds ticket, kind and material. The result reports `signing_key` (a `SHA256:` fingerprint) and the environment (principal). Trust bases after the change: `envelope_key` (validity-only) and `trust_list`; `own_key` and `pinned_environment` are removed.\n3. Optional per-environment trust list `verify.trusted_opcert_keys` (env override `REBAR_VERIFY_TRUSTED_OPCERT_KEYS`), entries `<env_id> <key-type> <base64> [comment]`, held in the verifying environment's own configuration (user config, env var, deployment, or a CI job's protected variables), never in the repository's `rebar.toml`. Listed (principal, key) passes with `trust_list`; a valid but unlisted pair is `untrusted`; a malformed list fails closed. No eras: rotation keeps retired keys listed while their certificates matter; on compromise the key is removed, which untrusts all its certificates.\n4. `verify.require_environment` keeps its meaning and now requires a trust list (a principal is a self-claim); without one it refuses with `foreign_key`. The merge gate `rebar verify-opcert` uses the gating environment's list; enabling enforcement means setting require_environment, opcert_enforce_since and the list in that environment.\n5. `.rebar/trusted_environments.yaml`, `rebar trusted-env`, and the pin file's log-position key eras are removed; signing never consults a list.\n6. Rebar's MCP server trusts only its own signatures: its deployment (`infra/compose/docker-compose.yml`, `infra/scripts/autodeploy.sh`) sets the 3 current keys, and that lands before the code stops reading the pin file.\nConsequences: developer clones, CI and review-bot become validity-only; certificates from other environments read `untrusted` on the MCP server; vocabulary changes (`untrusted`, `trust_list`, `signing_key`); rotation is a config change. Rejected alternatives: keep the repo pin file; per-key eras in the list; gating signing on a pinned key.\nMark ADR 0049 `Status: Accepted; repository key pinning superseded by ADR 0125`, and add a note pointing to ADR 0125 at ADR 0104 decision 3 and at its env_id note.\n\n## Scope\n- `docs/adr/0125-opcert-trust-per-environment.md` (new), `docs/adr/.numbers/0125` (new marker)\n- `docs/adr/README.md` (regenerated by `scripts/gen_adr_index.py`)\n- `docs/adr/0049-opcert-asymmetric.md` (status line only; body kept as the historical record)\n- `docs/adr/0104-mcp-on-box.md` (supersession notes at decision 3 and its env_id note)\nOut of scope: code, config, deployment and other docs (later stories of the epic).\n\n## Testing\nDocumentation-only; no new testable behavior. `scripts/check_adr_numbers.py` and the ADR index drift check run under `make verify`. Existing `tests/unit/test_opcert_adr_6a89.py` asserts ADR 0049 still contains `trusted_environments.yaml`, `rebar.opcert.v1` and `ADR 0046`; the body is kept. Portability: Markdown only.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] `docs/adr/0125-opcert-trust-per-environment.md` exists with Context, Decision, Consequences and Alternatives sections; its Decision states items 1-6 above, including `verify.trusted_opcert_keys`, `REBAR_VERIFY_TRUSTED_OPCERT_KEYS`, the entry format, `untrusted`, `trust_list`, `signing_key`, the no-eras rule, the require_environment rule, and the land-the-list-first ordering (checked by reading the merged file).\n- [ ] `docs/adr/.numbers/0125` exists and `python scripts/check_adr_numbers.py` exits 0.\n- [ ] Each of `docs/adr/0049-opcert-asymmetric.md` (status line), `docs/adr/0104-mcp-on-box.md` and `docs/adr/README.md` contains `0125` (checked: `git grep -c 0125 -- <file>` is at least 1 for each file).\n- [ ] `make verify` is green, and the change lands through Gerrit with LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1795114068522782581,"uuid":"d17d9ea4-b5fb-4850-b87d-b78ab072c7db"}